WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.520
- We need to be more 80/20,

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.420
the team is not working 80/20 enough.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.510
You need to approach this
analysis with the 80/20 mentality.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.550
So the 80/20 Principle or the 80/20 Rule

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.910
or the Pareto Principle, as
some people are calling it,

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.200
this is the topic for today's video.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.330
This principle or rule is so commonplace

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.470
in consulting and in many
other industries as well

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.750
that this term is almost a cliche.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.500
So as important as it is,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.780
I want you to really
understand what it's all about,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.120
why it's used so frequently in consulting

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.290
and what indeed the value of it all is.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:44.320
So let me start you by explaining
what this is all about.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.967
And indeed this Pareto Principle

00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:48.090
how it was originally called

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.750
was invented by Vilfredo Pareto.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.890
He was an Italian economist,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.890
and he apparently found out

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:59.180
that in Italy at the time 80% of the land

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.460
was owned by 20% of the people.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.600
So, 80/20, this relationship,
this is what it's all about.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.080
And this now permutes
in lots of other areas

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.370
of society, of work.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.610
And indeed the common
implication here for work

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.910
is that often for many many tasks,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.330
80% of the results

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:25.330
are created by 20% of
the activities, 80/20.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.330
So this is the topic that we
are going to talk about today.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.040
So, welcome to Firm Learning.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.860
My name is Heinrich Rusche
on my channel Firm Learning.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.430
I want to help you to become successful

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.740
in the first years of your career.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.450
I'm a former McKinsey
Management Consultant.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.870
And on my channel, I try to share my tips

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.280
both for soft skills and hard skills

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.200
that you need to be successful.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.850
So if you wanna hear more about that,

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.270
feel free to subscribe to the channel

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.450
to stay up to date on all my content.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.730
So indeed today's topic
is the Pareto Principle.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.120
I will look at it for two directions.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.440
The first is actually
the application of it

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:03.740
in analysis that you do,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.880
in practical work,

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.730
in your work as a consultant.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.150
So how can you leverage this principle

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.180
to use it for work that you
do together with the client?

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.013
But then second,

00:02:13.013 --> 00:02:16.610
I'm also going to talk about
how it's often used internally.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.200
So, let's dig into it to better understand

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.950
how the Pareto Principle really works.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.910
And what you see here is the typical curve

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.930
of how such a Pareto
relationship really looks like.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.140
And indeed the
characteristic of this curve

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.270
is that in the beginning,

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:35.040
the curve has a very high slope
that then reduces over time,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.780
goes down over time and
less it's almost zero

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:38.613
in the very end.

00:02:38.613 --> 00:02:40.270
But what do you actually see here?

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.750
So on the x-axis

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.450
you see the different elements,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.840
the different items, components,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.440
that then create a certain effect.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.340
And these components are
ordered by their size,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:51.840
or magnitude or impact, right?

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.080
So the first components are the strongest,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.540
biggest, best ones.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.010
And then the magnitude goes down.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.360
And then on the y-axis

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.050
you see the effect, the output,
the outcome of these inputs.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.570
And indeed what you can see here

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.130
is that the very first components,

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.900
create a significant
portion of the outcome,

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.820
and then every additional component,

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.290
every additional item that comes after it,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.520
it's marginal contribution
to the total results,

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.290
it's significantly diminishing.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:20.420
And in the end, it's almost zero.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.930
So, this was maybe a bit theoretical.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.350
So let's make it practical,

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.670
where is this really applied to practice?

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:26.810
Where does this really exist?

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.910
In our couple of examples from Wikipedia,

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.140
for instance, in the Wealth Distribution.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.710
So for many countries, the 80/20 Rule,

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.150
more or less is indicative

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.630
of the Wealth Distribution
of the population.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.950
So the wealthiest, the 20% of the people

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:44.250
own 80% of the total
wealth of the company.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.270
Another application,
the Healthcare systems.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.800
So, typically 20% of the patients

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.680
create 80% of the cost
of the healthcare system.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.250
In business, in my opinion
say it's 20% of the products

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.450
creating 80% of the revenue.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.020
And production, 20% of the parts

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.060
creating 80% of the
problem of the defaults.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.560
Or in buying, 20% of all your suppliers

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.930
are responsible for 80%
of your cost baseline.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.370
So these were just some examples
that are found on Wikipedia

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.870
and other places in the internet.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.320
Of course, this is never
precisely exactly the case,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.190
it just gives a rough indication.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:21.260
But at least personally,

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.483
I find it really fascinating to see

00:04:23.483 --> 00:04:26.880
that this is a relationship
that you really see often,

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.930
when you observe things
in the business world,

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.340
or in other areas of your daily life.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.420
So, how can you apply this 80/20 Principle

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.280
in your work as a consultant?

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.680
Also as an employee working
in a certain business function

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.900
if you just want to understand
your business a bit better?

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:43.810
Because indeed this Pareto Analysis

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.320
is an analysis that you can do

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.830
in many many different
types of circumstances.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.350
It's usually possible to
complete it with very basic data.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.220
And it at least provides
one angle, one perspective

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.230
at the structure of the business,

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.820
how certain things are working
together and are interlinked.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.330
So let me just share you one example.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.280
I was working in a sales project.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.650
And we're asking ourselves
how can we serve our customers

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:08.110
in a more cost-effective way?

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:09.660
So one of the first things that we did

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.570
is that we looked at the customer base

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:14.140
and we calculated the contribution margin

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.000
for each individual customer.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.290
So we were asking ourselves

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.940
how much does the company earned

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.820
with each of these individual customers?

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:23.690
And then we ordered the customers

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.140
from the highest to the
lowest contribution margin.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.380
And then we created the cumulate curve.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.460
And of course, no big surprise,

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.150
it was a Pareto relationship.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.870
Meaning that roughly the 20%

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.880
of the most profitable customers

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.620
contributed to 80% of the profits.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:41.060
And then actually in the end,

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:42.590
the curve even went down,

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.470
because there were a couple of customers

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.570
which had a negative contribution margin.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.140
So if you took the product margin

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.280
that was earned by selling
certain products to them,

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.280
and you subtracted

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.890
what the sales organization
was costing you.

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:55.250
So the cost to serve,

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:56.850
to actually serve these customers

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.000
and some of them were negative.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.960
And this was a great discussion starter,

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.060
with the head of sales

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.550
talking about how can
we further prioritize

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.750
these largest most important customers?

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:09.390
What ways can we think of
to reduce the cost of sales

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:10.540
for the longterm?

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.310
For the end of this whole curve?

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.980
Where at the moment we're
actually even losing money.

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:16.920
Also did a couple of purchasing projects.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.290
And here this is one of the first

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.670
standard analysis as well.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.360
So looking at the supplier base,
and you're asking yourself,

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.570
how was the spend of the company

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.100
distributed among the suppliers?

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.950
And again, often you also
see this strong concentration

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:32.720
often spend with a couple of suppliers

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.650
and then this big long tail

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.550
and you're often again, the question is,

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:37.810
how can we optimize these?

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.870
Can we consolidate some of
the spend of the suppliers

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.330
in the long tail, with larger suppliers

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:44.870
to simplify the organization

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.280
and potentially also get volume discounts.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.690
Next example, a project

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.350
where we looked into the
product portfolio of a company.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:53.580
Again, same thing Pareto analysis.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.270
You notice that a small
share of the products

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.900
create the vast majority of the revenue.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.560
And the question then is what can we do

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:01.393
with the long tail?

00:07:01.393 --> 00:07:03.540
Now, of course, do not
be misunderstood here.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.270
This Pareto analysis is a very basic tool,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.890
it's very quick and easy to
do it in the very beginning.

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.890
This will of course not
be the major insight

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:12.870
of such a project.

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.550
This will not be the end of it all.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.360
But it's often a good effect

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.520
to first starting point
to get for yourself also.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.090
Good understanding of how
this business is structured,

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:24.800
and potentially it can also
spark some first discussions

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.820
with your client
counterparts or colleagues

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:27.870
about certain topics,

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.240
that you now further want
to look into your project.

